{#
  Sub-navigation between the pages of ONE menu entry.

  It exists because a feature can be two pages without being two lines in
  the menu: a menu that grows a line per screen stops being a map. The
  entry names the feature; this names the screens inside it.

  Usage:
  {% include 'partials/sub_nav.html.twig' with {
      items: [
          { label: 'Tableau de bord', url: '/support-dashboard' },
          { label: 'Tickets', url: '/support-dashboard/tickets' },
      ],
      current: '/support-dashboard',
  } only %}

  `current` is compared exactly, so a sub-page with its own detail routes
  passes the parent's path rather than the request's — a detail screen
  belongs to the tab it was opened from, and a tab bar with nothing
  selected reads as broken.

  Not Bootstrap's `nav-tabs`: these navigate to real URLs rather than
  toggling panes, so they are links that look like tabs and never carry
  `role="tab"`, which would promise a keyboard behaviour they do not have.
#}
<nav class="mb-3" aria-label="Sections de cette page">
    <ul class="nav nav-pills gap-1">
        {% for item in items %}
            <li class="nav-item">
                <a class="nav-link{% if item.url == current %} active{% endif %}"
                   href="{{ item.url }}"
                   {% if item.url == current %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>
                    {{ item.label }}
                </a>
            </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>
</nav>
